# Can one ball become two? The Banach–Tarski paradox
tags: ["banach-tarski paradox","axiom of choice","measure theory","free groups","infinity"]
teaches: ["concepts/banach-tarski","concepts/axiom-of-choice","concepts/non-measurable-sets"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Sadaltager | a calm, warm mathematician who enjoys the puzzle; speaks at a slightly brisk, lively pace, never rushed","b":"gemini:Aoede | a relaxed, good-humoured co-host with a dry, understated wit; never exaggerated; speaks at a slightly brisk pace","take":"lines"}
prompt: "How can cutting and moving pieces double a ball? Why can't the pieces have a volume? Does this say something about the world, or about the axiom of choice?"
next: {"title":"Does the set of all sets that don't contain themselves contain itself? Russell's paradox","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/paradoxes/04-does-the-set-of-all-sets-that-don-t.cast"}

## Can one ball become two? The Banach–Tarski paradox
sources:
    - id: bt1924
      title: Sur la décomposition des ensembles de points en parties respectivement congruentes
      authors: Stefan Banach and Alfred Tarski
      year: 1924
      finding: 'A solid ball can be split into finitely many pieces and reassembled, by rigid motions, into two balls of the same size (Fundamenta Mathematicae 6: 244–277).'
    - id: robinson1947
      title: On the decomposition of spheres
      authors: Raphael M. Robinson
      year: 1947
      finding: Five pieces are enough to double a ball, and fewer will not do; one of the five can be a single point (Fundamenta Mathematicae 34).
    - id: wiki_bt
      title: Banach–Tarski paradox
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: Rotations by arccos(1/3) (≈ 70.5°) about two perpendicular axes generate a free group; the axiom of choice picks one point from each orbit; the pieces are non-measurable.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Banach%E2%80%93Tarski_paradox
    - id: banach1923
      title: Sur le problème de la mesure
      authors: Stefan Banach
      year: 1923
      finding: 'In the line and the plane there is a finitely additive measure on all sets, unchanged by rigid motions, so no such doubling exists there (Fundamenta Mathematicae 4: 7–33).'
    - id: cinq_lettres
      title: Cinq lettres sur la théorie des ensembles
      authors: René Baire, Émile Borel, Jacques Hadamard, Henri Lebesgue
      year: 1905
      finding: Borel, Baire and Lebesgue voiced reservations about Zermelo's 1904 use of arbitrary choices; Hadamard accepted it (Bulletin de la SMF 33).
    - id: solovay1970
      title: A model of set-theory in which every set of reals is Lebesgue measurable
      authors: Robert M. Solovay
      year: 1970
      finding: Without full choice (ZF + dependent choice, given an inaccessible cardinal), it is consistent that every set of reals is Lebesgue measurable (Annals of Mathematics 92).

