    card title "Can one ball become two?"

A: Here's  (@draw b0_g@)a claim that sounds like a magic trick: cut a solid ball int (@draw a0@)o a few pieces, and pu (@draw b1_g b2_g q0@)t them back together as two balls, each as big as the first.

A: Your first thought is surely right. Cutting and moving never makes mor (@draw vv@)e stuff: slice a cake, and the slices add up to one cake.
    highlight b0_g

A: Yet in 192 (@draw y1924@)4 two Polish mathematicians, Stefan Banac (@draw banach@)h and Alfred Tars (@draw tarski@)ki, proved that with the right pieces it can be done.

    erase banach tarski y1924

A: The rules are strict: no stretching, no ga (@draw rules@)ps, no overlaps, only sliding and turni (@draw cuts five@)ng. And five pieces are enough, one of the (@draw ctr@)m a single point.

A: Like Zeno's race and the full hotel, it's an argument where every step looks innocent and the end is impossible. So one step must be wrong.
    highlight vv

    erase rules

A: Here's the route: a list o (@draw r1@)f words that doubles itself, then the s (@draw r2@)ame trick on a ball, and last, the i (@draw r3@)nnocent step that gives way.

    pause 0.3

    clear

    card title "A list that doubles"

A: Start (@draw k_moves k_lab@) with four moves: left, right, up and down. A word is any strin (@draw k_ex k_ok@)g of them, like left, up, up.

A: One rule: never put a move right next to its undo. (@draw k_bad@) Left then right cance (@draw k_x k_badl@)ls out, so it's not allowed.

A: Now sort e (@draw rule2 h_L h_R h_U h_D@)very word by its first letter into four piles,  (@draw h_S w_S@)and keep the empty word, the start, on its own.

A: The  (@draw wl_L@)L pile holds L, LL, LU, LD, and so on forever. The other p (@draw wl_R wl_U wl_D parallel true@)iles look just the same.

A: Now the trick. Take the R pile and put a (@draw lpre@)n L in front of every word.
    highlight h_R effect underline

A: L th (@draw s0@)en R cancels. So R becomes the start, RR (@draw s1@) becomes R, RU be (@draw s2@)comes U, and RD  (@draw s3@)becomes D.

A: Every word that doesn't start with L turns up, exactly once. One pile has become three piles and the start.
    highlight s0 s1 s2 s3

    erase s0 s1 s2 s3 lpre

A: Add the untouched L pile, and t (@draw br1@)hat's every word there is, from just two of the four piles.

    erase k_moves k_lab k_ex k_ok k_bad k_x k_badl

A: That leaves the U and D piles. Can they make a second complete list?
    highlight col_U col_D

    ask "Put a D in front of every word in one pile, and it becomes every word not starting with D. Which pile? Tap it." choose ["col_L","col_R","col_U","col_D"] answer col_U store pile voice a
        right: The U pile: D undoes its first letter, and whatever followed is left.
        wrong: Which move does D undo?

A: The U pile. With the D pile as it was, that's a second complete list.
    draw d0 d1 d2 parallel true

    erase d0 d1 d2

A: One  (@draw br2@)list became two, by shifting, never stretching:  (@highlight br1 br2 duration 2.5@)the hotel trick, played with words.

    pause 0.4

    clear

    card title "Words on a ball"

A: Now pu (@draw sph sph_eq@)t the words on a ball. L and R turn it one way  (@draw ax_up ax_up_l turn_a@)or back about an upright axis; U and D tu (@draw ax_lv ax_lv_l@)rn it about a level one.

A: The angle is chosen oddl (@draw ang@)y, about seventy and a half degrees, so t (@highlight turn_a duration 2.5@)hat no string of turns ever undoes itself by accident.

A: Take  (@draw f1_p f1_p_l@)one point and follow it through every word:  (@draw f1_L f1_L_l@)L sends it her (@draw f1_U f1_U_l@)e, U here, and L th (@draw f1_LU f1_LU_l@)en U somewhere else again.

A: Its famil (@draw fam1more@)y is one point for each word: endless, but a list, like the rooms of the hotel.

    erase f1_L_l f1_U_l f1_LU_l

A: But a ball's surface has more points than any list can hold, Cantor's d (@draw fam2@)iagonal again. So there are endlessly many (@draw fam_l@) families, too many to list.

A: Here's the crucial step: from every family, (@draw st1@) pick one point (@draw st2@) as its start.

A: No rule says which point to pick, and there are far too many (@erase fam_l@) families to pick one by one. Mathematics allows it anyway:  (@draw zer@)Ernst Zermel (@draw aoc@)o used it in 1904 and made it an axiom in 1908, the axiom of choice.
    highlight st1 st2

A: Now every point has an address: its family's start, and the wo (@draw f1_LU_l2@)rd that leads there. Sort t (@draw pk_t pkey@)he points by the firs (@draw colour1@)t letter, and you have four pieces, with (@draw pk_s@) the starts as a spare one.

    erase f1_LU_l2

A: Turn the R piece by L, and just as with the (@draw s2_0 s2l_0@) words, it fills everything the L piece misses. U and D, tu (@draw s2_1 s2l_1@)rned the same way, make a second sphere.

A: For the (@draw rads@) solid ball, let each surface point carry its line to the centre. The cen (@draw cdot@)tre and a few points on the axes need one more hotel-style shift.

    pause 0.4

    clear

    card title "Where does it break?"

A: So which innocent step broke? Test the one nobody  (@draw assume@)questions: that every piece has a volume.

A: Call the ball's volume V. Th (@draw m1@)e four pieces fit inside one ball, so toge (@draw m1_n@)ther they're at most V.

A: But turning does (@draw m2_n@)n't change volume, and L and R rebuild a who (@draw m2@)le ball. So L plus R is V, and U plus D is V too.

    draw m3

    ask "Which tile makes the bottom line true? Tap it." on m3 others ["V","4V","0"] store tot voice a
        right: Two V: L and R make one ball, U and D another.
        wrong: Add the two lines just above.

A: Two V, and y (@draw m4@)et at most V. That on (@highlight m3 duration 1.5@)ly works if V is zero, and a ball's v (@draw m4_x@)olume isn't.

A: So the pieces  (@draw as_x@)have no volume. Not zero: no volum (@draw cloud@)e at all. They're clouds of points (@draw novol@), so finely mixed that 'how much space?' has no answer.

A: It needs three dimensions, too. In 1923 Banach  (@draw plane@)showed that in a flat plane, every shape keeps  (@highlight plane effect underline duration 2@)its area through any cutting and moving.

    pause 0.4

    clear

    card title "The world, or the axiom?"

A: Does any (@draw h_w rule5 div5 h_a@) of this say something about the world? Not really: real  (@draw atom w1@)balls are made of atoms, finitely many, and no knife can  (@draw w2@)cut a cloud of points picked by an axiom.

A: So the paradox is really about the axiom of choice, and there mathematicians disagreed.
    highlight h_a

A: Soon after Zer (@draw a1@)melo, Borel, Baire and Lebe (@draw a1n@)sgue doubted choices that no rule describes. For some, Banach–T (@highlight a1 duration 2@)arski is still a reason for doubt.

A: Most mathematicians tod (@draw a2@)ay accept the axiom, because so much else needs it. They read the par (@highlight a2 duration 2.5@)adox as a fact about volume: not every set of points has one.

A: And the other road is open. In 1970 Robert Solovay s (@draw a3@)howed that, without full choice, mathematics can consistent (@highlight a3 effect underline duration 2@)ly give every set of points a volume.

A: So the step that broke was the  (@draw broke@)innocent one: every piece has a volume. With the axiom of cho (@draw broke_x@)ice, some pieces simply don't.

    quiz "A friend splits the surface of a sphere into a few pieces and, only by turning them, builds two spheres the same size. What must be true of the pieces?" voice a
        right: Their area is undefined: if they had areas, those would add up to one sphere and to two at once.
        wrong: Think about what went wrong when we added up the volumes.
        * Each has zero area
        * Each is a single point
        + Their area is undefined

    clear

    card title "Can one ball become two?"

A: So,  (@draw b0_g a0 b1_g b2_g@)can one ball become two? On paper, yes, with f (@draw yes@)ive pieces nobody could ever cut.

A: In  (@draw no@)your hands, it (@fade b1_g b2_g a0 yes to 0.25@) stays one ball.

    highlight b0_g duration 1.5

## Where next
end_page: true

Next: Does the set of all sets that don't contain themselves contain itself? Russell's paradox
